1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,456
HERE uses a photo to find your position and guide your next step, without relying on GPS.

2
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:10,017
COLMAP builds a sparse map from a phone video.

3
00:00:10,017 --> 00:00:14,156
Reference cameras share one three dimensional coordinate system.

4
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:16,442
Upload a corridor photo.

5
00:00:16,442 --> 00:00:23,210
Feature matching and two D to three D registration estimate the camera position and orientation.

6
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:29,526
The result includes XYZ and camera orientation, with geometric quality checks.

7
00:00:29,526 --> 00:00:32,591
Metric distances require a measured scale.

8
00:00:33,208 --> 00:00:34,560
Choose a destination.

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,972
A star search follows the connected navigation graph.

10
00:00:37,972 --> 00:00:42,028
Turn directions come from the camera heading and route vectors.

11
00:00:42,625 --> 00:00:47,132
Photo guidance compares camera heading with the next route segment.

12
00:00:47,132 --> 00:00:51,168
Stored scene images help users recognize upcoming waypoints.

13
00:00:51,792 --> 00:00:53,873
Re-localize to update progress.

14
00:00:53,873 --> 00:01:01,327
For this controlled arrival check, we replay a registered waypoint image, then let the program confirm arrival.

15
00:01:01,917 --> 00:01:04,234
Photo localization comes first.

16
00:01:04,234 --> 00:01:09,468
Optional NFC tags could provide an extra check at known map locations.

17
00:01:10,083 --> 00:01:18,265
AI glasses offer a promising next step: hands free navigation, with local stories and cultural check ins along the way.

18
00:01:18,875 --> 00:01:25,200
A time lens could reveal how a place has changed, bringing local history into view as people explore.

19
00:01:25,792 --> 00:01:26,614
Photo first.

20
00:01:26,614 --> 00:01:28,876
Find your way, and discover more.
